//! `libio/stdio.c`: the standard streams `stdin`, `stdout` and `stderr`.
//!
//! [`stderr`] is C's standard error stream, lent as a
//! [`FileRef`](crate::libc::libio::genops::FileRef) for as long as its caller vouches that the
//! program keeps it open: glibc's `stderr` is a variable the program's C may set, and the
//! stream it names one that C may close. `stdin` and `stdout` are not bound yet.

pub use ffi_import::stderr;

#[expect(
    unsafe_code,
    reason = "reads glibc's `stderr` and borrows the stream it names"
)]
mod ffi_import {
    use ffibox::CRefSeam;

    use crate::libc::ffi;
    use crate::libc::libio::genops::FileRef;

    /// Wraps `glb::stderr`
    ///
    /// The standard error stream: the stream C's `stderr` names when the call reads it, as
    /// C's `stderr` expression does, lent for `'a`, the lifetime the caller chooses.
    ///
    /// glibc defines `stderr` with its stream's address, `_IO_2_1_stderr_`, a stream in
    /// static storage, unbuffered and open on descriptor 2, which is never freed: even
    /// `fclose` keeps the storage of a standard stream. No glibc routine assigns `stderr`
    /// (only its tests and its programs do, and a compatibility constructor for binaries
    /// linked against glibc 2.0, which runs before `main`). It is shared: every C stdio
    /// routine and every operation bound on [`FileRef`] holds its lock, as [`FileRef`]'s
    /// contract for a borrowed stream says.
    ///
    /// But glibc's `stderr` is a variable which a program "can set just like any others",
    /// as its manual says (*Standard Streams*), giving `fclose (stdout); stdout = fopen
    /// (…);` as the way to redirect a standard stream. A stream so opened is freed by the
    /// `fclose` that closes it. What the program's C does with `stderr` is beyond the
    /// crate's sight, so the caller vouches for it, for the handle's lifetime. A handle
    /// taken for one call, as C's `fprintf (stderr, …)` takes the stream, asks of the program
    /// what that C expression asks of it. One kept for the rest of the program, `'static`,
    /// asks that nothing ever closes it.
    ///
    /// A handle taken before C assigns `stderr` keeps the stream it was taken from.
    ///
    /// # Safety
    ///
    /// - No write of `stderr` races with this read: C assigns it only while no other thread may
    ///   read it.
    /// - The stream `stderr` names stays open for `'a`: nothing passes it to `fclose`, or to a
    ///   `freopen` that fails and leaves it closed, while the handle lives. A `freopen` that
    ///   succeeds keeps the stream open at its address, under its lock throughout.
    /// - Every other access to that stream while the handle lives, C's or Rust's, on any thread,
    ///   holds its lock: [`FileRef`]'s contract for a borrowed stream. Its locking stays internal,
    ///   glibc's default, so that nothing sets it to `FSETLOCKING_BYCALLER` with `__fsetlocking`,
    ///   and an `_unlocked` routine reaches it only under `flockfile`, as glibc's own `error` does.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// If C has set `stderr` to NULL, which no C of glibc or of either parent does.
    #[must_use]
    pub unsafe fn stderr<'a>() -> FileRef<'a> {
//! `stdio-common/fprintf.c`: `fprintf`, the formatted write to a C stream.
//!
//! Rust formats, and C writes: [`FileRef::print`] hands text Rust has already formatted
//! to `fprintf(stream, "%s", text)`, so that it lands in the stream's own buffer, in order
//! with what the C that shares the stream writes, and [`FileRef::write_fmt`] lets `write!`
//! format into it. printf's conversions are not reached: the text is never a format, so
//! a `%` in it is written as it is.
//!
//! [`FileRef::print`]: crate::libc::libio::genops::FileRef::print
//! [`FileRef::write_fmt`]: crate::libc::libio::genops::FileRef::write_fmt

#[expect(
    unsafe_code,
    reason = "calls flockfile, fprintf and funlockfile on a borrowed stream"
)]
mod ffi_import {
    use core::ffi::{CStr, c_int};
    use core::fmt;
    use std::ffi::CString;
    use std::io;

    use ffibox::CRefSeam;

    use crate::libc::csu::errno_loc::{errno, set_errno};
    use crate::libc::ffi;
    use crate::libc::libio::genops::FileRef;

    impl FileRef<'_> {
        /// Wraps `fn::fprintf`
        ///
        /// Writes `text` to the stream, as `fprintf(stream, "%s", text)` does, and returns the
        /// number of bytes written, `text.len()`.
        ///
        /// The write holds the stream's lock from start to end, taken with `flockfile`, whatever
        /// the stream's locking mode. A stream C set to `FSETLOCKING_BYCALLER` is locked too,
        /// although glibc's own `fprintf` would skip the lock on it. So one call's text is
        /// never interleaved with another thread's write to the stream, and the call waits
        /// while another thread holds the lock. The calling thread may already hold it, as
        /// the lock is recursive. A stream that has no lock is refused, as below.
        ///
        /// The text lands in the stream's buffer, and reaches the file when the buffering mode
        /// the C gave the stream says, or at `fflush` or `fclose`.
        ///
        /// # Errors
        ///
        /// - [`io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput`], when `text` holds a NUL byte, which a C string cannot
        ///   carry. Nothing is written, and `errno` is left as it was.
        /// - [`io::ErrorKind::Unsupported`], when the stream has no lock for `flockfile` to take:
        ///   the stream glibc hands the handler of a conversion that `register_printf_specifier`
        ///   adds, over printf's own buffer, whose lock is NULL. Nothing is written, and `errno` is
        ///   left as it was.
        /// - `errno`'s error, when `fprintf` fails and sets it: `EBADF` for a stream not open for
        ///   writing, which sets its error indicator and has nothing written to it; the error of
        ///   the write that flushes the buffer, such as `ENOSPC`, `EPIPE` or `EIO`, after part of
        ///   `text` may have reached the stream; and `EOVERFLOW` for a `text` longer than `INT_MAX`
        ///   bytes, whose count `fprintf` cannot return, though the text is written. `errno` keeps
        ///   that error, as C's does.
        /// - [`io::ErrorKind::Other`], with no OS error, when `fprintf` fails without setting
        ///   `errno`. A stream that is wide-oriented (by `fwide` or a wide-character write) does
